PostgreSQL schema migrations for Enterprise AI QA Agent.

20261002_0001 covers the durable task outbox and its two pending indexes.
It bootstraps a fresh table or adds claim columns to the supported legacy table.
20261002_0002 adds tool jobs, tool artifacts and their four existing lookup indexes.
It supports fresh tables and existing tables with the current supported shape;
it does not infer missing fields/types for arbitrary historical schemas.
Existing rows, IDs, payloads, attempts, evidence and publication state are preserved.
This is not a baseline for the remaining application schemas. Store initializers
remain active until their own frozen revisions and deployment policy are added.

From Agent_Server, with the same PostgreSQL configuration as the application:

    uv sync --extra dev

The dev extra declares Alembic (verified with 1.20.0, MIT, Python >=3.10,
SQLAlchemy >=2.0). Review the resolved environment before upgrading a deployment.
Then inspect the current version and the SQL without modifying business tables:

    uv run --no-sync alembic current
    uv run --no-sync alembic heads
    uv run --no-sync alembic upgrade head --sql

Review the offline SQL and back up the database before a deployment operator runs:

    uv run --no-sync alembic upgrade head

Only current/heads/offline SQL are read-only. Do not stamp head to pretend that
unmigrated application tables are covered, and do not call runtime initializers
from a revision. Revision SQL is frozen; later schema changes need new revisions.

Online migrations use the actual SQLAlchemy URL object (not a diagnostic URL with
a hidden password), UTC connections and a transaction-scoped advisory lock per
database/current schema. Alembic Config.attributes['connection'] allows a caller
to supply its own transaction; Config.attributes['settings'] supplies the usual
table configuration. The caller owns such a connection and its commit/rollback.
Loading the migration logging configuration leaves application loggers enabled.

Both revisions intentionally refuse destructive downgrade because queued work
and evidence can predate the migration. Use a reviewed backup restore/forward repair for
rollback; an offline downgrade is not a data-preserving recovery plan.

OrmBase has no mapped tables. Autogenerate is disabled rather than comparing an
empty metadata against a populated database and proposing table drops. Add
explicit reviewed revisions for the raw SQL stores.

Verification uses random PostgreSQL schemas and actual Alembic commands:

    uv run --no-sync python scripts/run_test_quality_gate.py --profile migration-core

It does not upgrade or stamp application-owned tables. The quality report pins
alembic/env.py, revisions and alembic.ini in addition to the application/test
sources. Real production upgrade, all store schemas, MySQL and coordinated
runtime-DDL removal are still separate work.
